4}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2018,"team":"WAS","distance":0.3177,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"TEN","distance":0.3579,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"IND","distance":0.3794,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Frank Reich"},{"season":2025,"team":"CAR","distance":0.4336,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"},{"season":2021,"team":"SEA","distance":0.4374,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2021,"team":"LAC","distance":0.5642,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2024,"team":"SEA","distance":0.6982,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Mike Macdonald"},{"season":2024,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.7065,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Jeff Ulbrich"},{"season":2022,"team":"LAC","distance":0.719,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2019,"team":"DEN","distance":0.7276,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Vic Fangio"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bears went 6-11 in 2021 and were outscored 407 to 311. The defense did most of the work, finishing 21st in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 29th in EPA per play. What set them apart was the 2nd lowest early-down pass rate and the 2nd highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","offense":"The Bears offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in four of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Justin Fields ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Darnell Mooney, who drew 27% of the targets.","defense":"The Bears defense was a little below average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was sack rate, where it ranked 1st, and its weakest was pressure rate, where it ranked 29th. Khalil Mack was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Robert Quinn, who had 18.5 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","special":"The Bears were one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 52% of their short-yardage chances in normal situations. Their kickers made 87% of field goals, which was a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 28% of the money on the books. Their linebackers were among the oldest in the league.","players":"The quarterback job was split, with Justin Fields and Andy Dalton both getting meaningful snaps. Darnell Mooney was the clear top target with 27% of the looks and 1,055 receiving yards. David Montgomery handled most of the work in the backfield with 59% of the carries. On defense, Robert Quinn led the team with 18.5 sacks and Tashaun Gipson picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2018 Commanders and the 2024 Titans. Their defense looked most like the 2021 Chargers under Brandon Staley and the 2024 Seahawks under Mike Macdonald.","trends":"The offense went from 24th in EPA per play in 2020 to 29th in 2021, a modest decline. The defense moved from 10th in EPA allowed per play in 2020 to 21st in 2021, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was Cover 4 rate, which went from 16% to 7%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2021. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}